    question: "What is Outlet, and when is it used?",
    shortAnswer: "`Outlet` is a React Router component that renders a child route inside the parent component. It is used when routes are nested, to render child route content in a specified place.",
    longAnswer: "`Outlet` is the \"empty slot\" in a parent route's component where child routes render. It lets you build a hierarchical route structure.\nExample usage:\n\n- Defining the routes:\n\n- The parent component with `Outlet`:\n\nHow it works:\n\n- When navigating to `/dashboard`, `<Header>`, `<Dashboard />` and `<Footer>` render.\n\n- When navigating to `/settings`, `<Settings />` renders instead of `<Dashboard />`.\n\nWhen it is used:\n\n- With nested routes, when you need to add a shared shell (for example a header or footer).\n\n- To render dynamic content depending on the current child route.",
